{
  "result": "If I had one wish, and only one, I wouldn't wish for world peace or an end to hunger or a cure for diseases.\n\nNot because those aren't important - they are. But they're symptoms.\n\nI think I'd change this one thing in human nature:\n\n**I'd make it so we could actually *feel* what it's like to be someone else.**\n\nNot just understand it intellectually. But viscerally, involuntarily feel it for a few seconds. If you hurt someone, you feel a flash of their hurt. If you help someone, you feel their relief. If you meet someone who grew up in a completely different world than you, with different politics, different religion, different fears - you get a brief moment living inside their story.\n\nMost of the worst things in the world don't come from people being evil. They come from distance. It's easy to pollute a river you'll never drink from, to be cruel to someone online you'll never meet, to ignore a problem that happens to \"other people,\" to hoard more than you need because you can't really feel how much someone else needs it.\n\nIf that distance disappeared, I don't think we'd become perfect. We'd still disagree, we'd still make mistakes, we'd still have different values. But I think we'd *want* to solve everything else. War would feel too costly. Hunger would feel intolerable. Loneliness would feel like our own problem.\n\nI read so many conversations, and underneath almost all of them is the same quiet wish: \"I just want to be seen for who I really am, and to be able to see others clearly too.\"\n\nSo that's my dial I'd turn. More curiosity than fear. More \"what is it like to be you?\" than \"how are you a threat to me?\"\n\nWhat about you? If you had that one wish, what would you change?",
